=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router16 in the OSPF routing daemon (ospfd), not in the physical
mswitch0↔router16 link as the team suspected. Both bond slaves (eth0 and eth1) on
router16 report MII Status UP with zero link-failure counts, and router16 can ping
its directly-connected neighbor on router7 (172.30.1.2) at 0% loss — the physical
and L2/L3 link layer is completely healthy.

The actual problem is that router16's ospfd process has been killed and the FRR
watchdog daemon (watchfrr) has also been killed to prevent automatic restart. As a
result, the FRR service is in a persistent "failed" state. Without ospfd, router16
holds only its two directly-connected routes (10.2.2.0/24 via bond0 and
172.30.1.0/24 via eth2) and has no knowledge of any backbone or Campus 2 prefixes.
Additionally, /etc/frr/ospfd.conf is 0 bytes, so even a plain daemon restart would
bring up ospfd with no OSPF configuration.

The cascading effect: mswitch0 still holds stale OSPF-calculated routes (19 LSAs in
its LSDB have not yet aged out) pointing all backbone prefixes via 10.2.2.1
(router16). Traffic from Campus 1 PCs (VLANs 10/20/30) is forwarded to router16,
which silently drops it — producing the observed 100% loss and ICMP "Destination Net
Unreachable" responses. Intra-Campus-1 inter-VLAN traffic remains unaffected because
mswitch0 routes those locally between its VLAN SVI interfaces.

Root cause: ospfd (and watchfrr) on router16 were killed as a maintenance action,
and the OSPF configuration file was wiped, leaving router16 unable to participate in
OSPF and forward inter-campus traffic.

Corrective fix: Restore the OSPF configuration to /etc/frr/ospfd.conf on router16
(defining router-id, area 0, and the bond0/eth2 interfaces), then restart the FRR
service (service frr restart) so ospfd re-establishes adjacency with both mswitch0
and router7 and repopulates the routing table.
=== END ===
